MARA IVES

Regulatory memory auditor. Protagonist.

Private ritual (weird but human)
Before and after every deep immersion, she smells a small sealed vial containing neutral air captured from an empty room. She uses it to “reset” her emotional baseline, as if returning to a place where nothing has happened yet.

Moral line she won’t cross (or thinks she won’t)
She will not knowingly certify a memory she believes is emotionally misrepresented, even if it is legally compliant.

The worst thing she’s done (kept secret)
Years ago, she approved a stabilization adjustment on a minor licensed memory to help a struggling origin owner pass renewal. It altered nothing structurally but slightly softened distress markers. She told herself it was within tolerance. It changed how the memory is now widely experienced.

What she would die for
The chance for a person to fully own at least one memory that no one else has shaped.

Voice sample
“Consistency is not the same as truth, but it’s what we know how to measure.”

DANIEL ROE

Original owner of the licensed comfort memory.

Private ritual (weird but human)
He rewrites short descriptions of the same childhood moment every morning from memory, never looking at previous versions. He keeps the pages but never rereads them.

Moral line he won’t cross (or thinks he won’t)
He will not knowingly license a memory he believes causes harm to renters.

The worst thing he’s done (kept secret)
He once increased licensing exposure beyond recommended limits during a financial emergency, accelerating emotional layering. That decision likely contributed to the memory becoming unusually regulating.

What he would die for
The continued care of the one person whose safety he believes depends on the income from his memory.

Voice sample
“If people feel steadier inside something I lived, doesn’t that mean it’s still doing what it was meant to do?”

LILA CHEN

Mara’s colleague. High-frequency memory subscriber.

Private ritual (weird but human)
She catalogs emotional states like weather reports in a personal log: pressure level, duration, recovery time. She never uses words like happy or sad. Only system language.

Moral line she won’t cross (or thinks she won’t)
She will not access unlicensed or black market memories, no matter how destabilized she feels.

The worst thing she’s done (kept secret)
During a severe withdrawal period, she scheduled overlapping immersion sessions under different subscription categories to bypass exposure limits. She told regulators it was a scheduling error.

What she would die for
Predictable internal quiet. Not happiness. Not meaning. Just non-volatility.

Voice sample
“I’m not dependent, I’m regulated. Those are different conditions with different treatment plans.”

DR. ARUN VELEZ

Clinical researcher studying emotional dependency in licensed memory users.

Private ritual (weird but human)
He records his own biometric data during emotionally significant conversations, then later tries to recall what the person said without checking the recording, comparing physiology to memory accuracy.

Moral line he won’t cross (or thinks he won’t)
He will not suppress or falsify data, regardless of institutional pressure.

The worst thing he’s done (kept secret)
He delayed publishing early withdrawal severity findings because the dataset was incomplete, even though he suspected immediate disclosure might have triggered protective policy sooner.

What he would die for
Proof that emotional reliance on shared experiences is medically real and must be treated as such.

Voice sample
“Subjective distress becomes ethically actionable the moment it becomes physiologically predictable.”
